===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{&lit|en|pull|out}}
#: {{ux|en|I need to '''pull''' the splinter '''out''' of my hand.}}
# {{lb|en|also|figurative|intransitive}} To [[withdraw]]; especially of [[military]] [[forces]]; to [[retreat]].
#: {{ux|en|The troops '''pulled out''' of the conflict.}}
#: {{ux|en|The mayor '''pulled out''' of the race for Senate after numerous opinion polls had him polling at less than 10 percent.}}
#: {{ux|en|The racehorse '''pulled out''' of the Stakes with a hurt foot.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/international/archive/2017/05/paris-agreement-trump/528621/|title=Leaving the Paris Agreement Would Be Indefensible|work=The Atlantic|date=2017-05-31|author=Todd Stern|passage='''Pulling out''' of Paris would cause serious diplomatic damage.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 11 2022|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2022/mar/11/chelsea-in-crisis-but-there-is-no-will-to-leave-club-on-their-knees-roman-abramovich|author=David Hytner|title=Chelsea are in crisis but there is no will to leave club on their knees|journal=w:The Guardian
|passage=There is still time to find a buyer and for them to stabilise the operation, especially if the deal were done by 31 May – when the club’s special licence to carry on is due to expire. There remain plenty of interested parties, who can only see Chelsea’s price dropping as sponsors '''pull out''' or consider their associations; as revenue streams are hit.}}
# {{lb|en|aviation|intransitive|of an aircraft}} To transition from a [[dive]] to [[level]] or [[climb]]ing [[flight]].
#: {{ux|en|After releasing its bomb, the plane '''pulled out''' of its dive.}}
# {{lb|en|literally|intransitive}} To use [[coitus interruptus]] as a method of [[birth control]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=David J. Clayton|title=The Healthy Guide to Unhealthy Living: How to Survive Your Bad Habits|publisher=Simon and Schuster|isbn=9780743295901|page=110|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=D82NNVio-6MC&pg=PA110&dq=%22pull+out%22&hl=&cd=5&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22pull%20out%22%&f=false|text=Shortly before you ejaculate, you can '''pull out''' and use your hand (or hers) to push yourself over the edge. Some of my patients claim this is a more natural method of birth control{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Grace Burrowes|title=Once Upon a Tartan|year=2013|publisher=Sourcebooks|page=287|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=vfVbAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA287&dq=%22pull+out%22&hl=&cd=1&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22pull%20out%22&f=false|isbn=978-1-4022-6870-0|passage=She'd long since caught the knack of moving with him, and closed her arms and legs around him. “You'll fly with me, Tiberius? Take the last fence with me?” He'd meant to '''pull out'''. ''Coitus interruptus'' was a term even the scholars failing their Latin knew before they left public school.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|transitive}} To [[remove]] something from a [[container]].
#: {{synonyms|en|whip out|draw}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''pulled''' his gun '''out''' before she had a chance to scream.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic|intransitive}} To [[maneuver]] a vehicle from the side of a road onto the [[lane]].
#: {{ux|en|When joining a road, you should check for traffic before '''pulling out'''.}}
# To [[draw out]] or [[lengthen]].

